00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.460
So we're celebrating music's
connection to civil rights.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.310
So who were your social
musical inspirations?

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.290
- Nina Simone and Aretha Franklin.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.090
- [Kelly] Oh yeah, good.
(audience clapping)

00:01:13.397 --> 00:01:16.290
- When I found out, actually
whilst I was learning about

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.910
both of them, 'cause I sing them a lot.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.823
I sing their music a lot,
is that Aretha was inspired

00:01:21.823 --> 00:01:26.610
by Nina Simone and she ended
up doing a couple of the songs

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.790
that Nina Simone had on her
albums. "Young Gift and Black."

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.623
- Yeah.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:33.420
- You know, and, and
obviously Nina was very much

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.780
in the civil rights Movement.
Her music was about that

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.930
at some point and they
sort of met in the middle.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.510
And both of those women are
huge inspirations for me.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:46.110
- Yeah, definitely. It's
interesting 'cause nobody thinks

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.390
about it, but one, what
was like the first time

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.942
you heard Aretha? Like the first question.

00:01:50.942 --> 00:01:53.730
- I think I was, I wanna say
it was maybe 10 years old.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:54.563
- Okay.

00:01:54.563 --> 00:01:56.640
- In my mother's car on the way to school.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.860
And I think it was "Chain of Falls."

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.630
- Yeah. I think mine was around 13

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.210
and it was "Respect" or "Chain
of Falls." It's one of them.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.230
- [Cynthia] One of them.
- Can't remember which one.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.060
But I just, there was something
different about her voice.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.050
- Isn't it just like-
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.730
- It just sort of like hits you?

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:09.563
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:09.563 --> 00:02:10.650
- Like in the right, in the solar plexus.

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:11.590
- Like just breaks you.
- [Cynthia] Yeah.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:12.720
- Yeah.
- [Cynthia] Yeah.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.330
- And the tone. But nobody
really talks about this.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.365
But she actually did cover
quite a bit of music.

00:02:17.365 --> 00:02:18.315
- Yes, she did. Yes she did.

00:02:18.315 --> 00:02:19.148
- Like that was a lot of her career,

00:02:19.148 --> 00:02:19.981
which nobody really talked

00:02:19.981 --> 00:02:22.350
because I feel like that's
maybe what influenced me to do,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.183
like covers all the time.
- [Cynthia] Yeah.

00:02:23.183 --> 00:02:24.653
- Because I love it so
much and I love genres.

00:02:24.653 --> 00:02:28.650
- She had this wonderful
magical way of making any song

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.380
you may have heard that
belonged to someone else,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.070
sound like it was already written for her.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:34.903
- I thought that.

00:02:34.903 --> 00:02:35.736
- And they were just a
stepping stone to get to her.

00:02:35.736 --> 00:02:36.657
- So when I was a kid,
I would listen to her

00:02:36.657 --> 00:02:37.830
and I was like, oh, I love her saying,

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.120
they're like, well, you
know, that's a cover.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.732
Like, they did that originally.
I was like, wait, what?

00:02:40.732 --> 00:02:41.565
- [Cynthia] Yeah, "Respect" is a cover.

00:02:41.565 --> 00:02:42.398
- And I was like, that's what I'm saying.

00:02:42.398 --> 00:02:44.760
I never knew like all those,
I ended up finding that out

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.320
and I was like, oh, I didn't.
- Oh, didn't know.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.090
- [Kelly] Gosh. Yeah.
- Right. Special.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:49.830
- Nobody wanted her to cover the song.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.480
- Oh, no, no.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.313
- 'Cause then it was gone.
- [Cynthia] You lost it. Yeah.

00:02:52.313 --> 00:02:54.060
- It was not yours anymore.
- Yeah.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.400
- But do you hear from young fans,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.100
like who've inspired you
as they inspired you?

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.180
- Young fans who have inspired?

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.040
- Yeah. Like how Nina
and Aretha inspired you?

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:02.873
Do you hear of young fans?

00:03:02.873 --> 00:03:03.706
- [Cynthia] Me inspiring them?

00:03:03.706 --> 00:03:04.539
- Yeah. Absolutely. What?

00:03:04.539 --> 00:03:06.870
- Oh, I mean, I hear it. I do hear it.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:10.200
And it always is a little bit overwhelming

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.340
'cause I feel like I've just begun.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.310
You know what I mean? I feel
like I still have much to give

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.807
and hopefully more to learn as well.

00:03:16.807 --> 00:03:19.170
- Yeah. But you're already so incredible.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.795
- Thank you for saying that. Thank you.

00:03:20.795 --> 00:03:22.949
- You're literally like in
my in ears, we sang together.

00:03:22.949 --> 00:03:25.806
(audience applauding)

00:03:25.806 --> 00:03:30.540
I've never, you have a
flawless transition voice.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.000
Like you transitioned to
every different octave

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.550
of your voice, flawlessly.
- Thank you.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:37.406
- It was intimidating as
hell to sing with you.

00:03:37.406 --> 00:03:38.270
- No! Come on now.
- I was like-

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.320
- It is. It's one of my
favorite things I've ever done.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.630
But just to hear you in
my in ears is a blessing.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:43.463
You are so good.

00:03:43.463 --> 00:03:44.340
- Thank you very much for saying that.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.173
- [Kelly] Yeah. Well, I'm not lying.

00:03:45.173 --> 00:03:47.430
- You're incredible. I mean
that your voice is astounding

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.213
and I know you're not lying
'cause you don't have to.

00:03:49.213 --> 00:03:51.300
- I would not say it. Yeah. And you are.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.340
I literally, I think
Jesse and I was talking,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.595
I was like, well, that's
what a singer sounds like.

00:03:55.595 --> 00:03:58.288
Yeah. I was like, wow.
Well, speaking of fans,

00:03:58.288 --> 00:04:00.270
so we came across this video,
(audience applauding)

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:03.273
Cynthia singing on the street.
So let's see it and hear it.

00:04:04.509 --> 00:04:09.509
♪ I'll march my band out ♪

00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:14.579
♪ I'll beat my drum ♪

00:04:14.579 --> 00:04:18.469
♪ And if I'm fanned out ♪

00:04:18.469 --> 00:04:20.099
- So good.
(audience applauding)

00:04:20.099 --> 00:04:21.528
- Funny girl. Obviously.
- Yeah.

00:04:21.528 --> 00:04:22.996
- [Kelly] I love funny girl.
- Yeah.

00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:24.733
- So that says eight years. Yeah.

00:04:24.733 --> 00:04:26.940
I love that whole music world.
But what happened there?

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.590
How did you just start
singing that? I love you.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.930
- Well.
- You're just like,

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:31.980
you know what I think? I feel like.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:32.813
- We're in the middle of the street.

00:04:32.813 --> 00:04:34.320
I bumped into him in
the middle of the street

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.440
and he asked me to sing something.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.260
I think he must have heard
me sing it one time before.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.030
And he was like, can you
just do a little bit of it?

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.130
Can you just? And so I
was like, okay, sure.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.440
I don't mind any opportunity
where I could make some music.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.000
That's what I'm gonna do.
- Oh, I don't either. Yeah.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.860
People always, that's
like, people come up to me

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.970
and be like, oh, I don't know

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.080
if you want me to talk
about American Idol.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.780
I'm like, hell yeah dude.
That's like why I'm here. Yeah.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.795
- It's also like, there's this you know.

00:04:56.795 --> 00:04:57.670
(audience cheering)

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.156
- You're gonna care when
they don't want you to sing.

00:04:59.156 --> 00:05:00.253
- That's the thing.
- Yeah.

00:05:00.253 --> 00:05:02.619
- It's like, I think the
wonderful thing about that,

00:05:02.619 --> 00:05:04.920
I knew it would bring him such joy

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.533
and he's such a joyous
energy and I wanted.

00:05:06.533 --> 00:05:08.160
- And you made his day.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.710
- It was so awesome and
I was having a great time

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.190
and people across the street could see it.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:13.260
Like, it was just fun.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.390
- And you're reminded right,
of like why you do it.

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:16.223
- Do it in the first place.

00:05:16.223 --> 00:05:17.056
- How you fell in love with it.

00:05:17.056 --> 00:05:17.889
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:05:17.889 --> 00:05:19.166
- Yeah.
- Those are important moments.

00:05:19.166 --> 00:05:20.760
New film is called "Drift."
(audience applauding)

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:24.010
You can see it in theaters
nationwide. February 16th.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.096
Today we're celebrating social movements

00:05:26.096 --> 00:05:28.590
that music has touched or inspired.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:32.100
So even in our personal lives,
we use music to set the tone

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.210
for whatever kind of mood we're in

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:34.230
or whatever's going on our lives.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.150
So what's on your like, latest playlist?

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.790
Like what's inspiring you?
- Latest playlist.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.120
Oh, there's a song by a
new artist called "Manana."

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.776
And it's called "You First."

00:05:44.776 --> 00:05:46.740
- [Kelly] Ooh, that's, I like the title.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:51.090
- Oh, it's brilliant. It is
about, I guess this is a couple

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.090
that might have broken up and he says,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.400
I'll do this if you do that first.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.420
So I'll stop saying, I'll stop joking.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.707
'Cause you don't like my
jokes, but you stop first.

00:06:02.707 --> 00:06:04.976
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Or I'll smile for you.

00:06:04.976 --> 00:06:05.838
- [Kelly] Who's gonna make the move?

00:06:05.838 --> 00:06:06.907
- Who's gonna make the move first?

00:06:06.907 --> 00:06:07.740
- Yeah.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:08.878
- And I think it's one of
the most beautiful songs

00:06:08.878 --> 00:06:11.160
I've ever heard.
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:11.993
- Yeah.

00:06:11.993 --> 00:06:13.524
- Again, the new movie is called "Drift."

00:06:13.524 --> 00:06:14.357
- Yes.

00:06:14.357 --> 00:06:15.870
- So kind of tell us what it's
about. Who's your character?

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.510
- So it's about a young
lady called Jacqueline

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:22.470
who is displaced because
of the war in Liberia.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:25.530
And she finds herself alone in Greece.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.750
And she's, you follow her
just trying to put the pieces

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:32.160
of her life back together,
you know, it's tough for her.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.873
But she tries to make
every day the next day.

00:06:38.692 --> 00:06:42.330
And she meets a tour guide
who is sort of in her own way

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.430
also trying to put her life back together.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.470
And they find friendship within each other

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.690
and something in the other
person breaks Jacqueline open

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:54.270
and she finally can begin to heal. Yeah.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.730
- I love that 'cause it also
expresses like the seasons

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.438
of friendships and people that
come in and out of your life.

00:06:58.438 --> 00:06:59.271
- [Cynthia] Yeah.

00:06:59.271 --> 00:07:00.600
- And I feel like people come in and out

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.086
for different reasons.
- [Cynthia] Yeah. For reasons.

00:07:02.086 --> 00:07:06.870
- Ah, well here's the thing.
"It would be" is the song

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.030
that you and who wrote it with you?

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.710
- Laura and Vula, we
co-wrote song together.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.530
- Oh my gosh. I was
telling her in the break,

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.320
I was sent this song while
I was out in New York

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.180
just running errands and doing stuff.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.580
And I teared up, I cried. I was like,

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:23.250
this is one of the best songs I've heard

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.798
in a very long time.
Like, it is so beautiful.

00:07:25.798 --> 00:07:27.510
(audience clapping)

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:30.690
The production is so beautifully sparse.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:34.496
The vocal ability. You are crazy rude.

00:07:34.496 --> 00:07:36.270
Like crazy rude.
(Cynthia laughing)

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:38.730
It's just insane how fluid it is.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:40.653
I've never heard anything like it.

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:44.760
The messaging is so great
and you lift it up, you know,

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.080
you don't hide it with much friction.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.480
You just lift up the
actual purpose of the song.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.970
Like what was that like, the
songwriting process for that?

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.980
- I was really moved by the whole thing.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:55.710
Just because Laura is
such a wonderful friend.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.510
I think she's unbelievably talented.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.910
And it happened because I was on a run

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.590
whilst we were shooting the movie.

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:04.590
And I make playlists
for the movies that I do

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:06.523
or the TV shows that whatever.

00:08:06.523 --> 00:08:08.820
Just so that I can find
myself in the the space.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:09.653
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:09.653 --> 00:08:12.930
- And I put one of her songs
on my playlist called "Father."

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.060
And as it was playing, it felt like

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.590
it was the language of the piece.

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.240
It felt like it spoke the
language of the piece.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:19.500
And I was like, oh my goodness,

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.090
I think I wanna write the song for this

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.770
and I wanna do it with this person.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:25.770
So I text Laura and I was
like, would you please.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:26.603
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:26.603 --> 00:08:27.630
- Would you please do this song with me?

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:31.380
And I sent her the material.
I sent her like clips

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.370
of the film and I sent her
the script and she said yes.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.060
So we then took a couple
of nights and went away

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:43.350
and wrote together and we
vocalled it in a couple days.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:47.550
And that's what came of
it. And she just was,

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.260
I guess she said she was inspired

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.020
by the water in the piece and the story

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.726
and she just got it. And I was.

00:08:54.726 --> 00:08:56.670
- Boy did you both. Yeah.
- [Cynthia] Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:59.820
- It was, I literally
listened and I was like,

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.758
I felt like I'd never
written a song before.

00:09:01.758 --> 00:09:02.591
(Cynthia laughing)

